  ],
  "exact_regions": [
    {
      "id": "A2.E1",
      "start_utf8_byte": 717553,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 719703,
      "slice_bytes": 2150,
      "slice_sha256": "1f2c060d3d5cadb913d3707396a03d12fcbee241933e4bdc134beac774b8f18f",
      "text": "(\\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int f(x^{\\prime},y)\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})=\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y=y]\\,. (B.1)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E2",
      "start_utf8_byte": 720477,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 724025,
      "slice_bytes": 3548,
      "slice_sha256": "2a3ed85e38715b4d6c91c8dbfb4a485c159c4511ad42beaded2a796d4bcdf30b",
      "text": "\\begin{split}\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\\text{ for some }g\\in L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}^{Y})\\}\\,,\\\\ \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\ker(\\mathsf{P})=\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y]=0\\}\\,.\\end{split} (B.2)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E3",
      "start_utf8_byte": 728702,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 732443,
      "slice_bytes": 3741,
      "slice_sha256": "7cb41b24f384991a849eef67e263bfcf9b3b2903b8a6c8dbef78020da9beb8b2",
      "text": "\\mathsf{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}&\\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\perp}\\\\ \\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}&\\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\perp}\\end{pmatrix}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\begin{aligned} \\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}\\,,&\\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\,,\\\\ \\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}\\,,&\\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\,.\\end{aligned} (B.3)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E4",
      "start_utf8_byte": 734351,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 735917,
      "slice_bytes": 1566,
      "slice_sha256": "bd93f2e460e4f59aa562b2ba13e36ad16cd810424677c8b114e6a93474fc65b8",
      "text": "(\\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq f(\\mathsf{U}(x,y))=f(x,2x-y)\\,. (B.4)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E5",
      "start_utf8_byte": 740263,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 742291,
      "slice_bytes": 2028,
      "slice_sha256": "fe258f4f7d272a57a2fa1293a3b4663924b5a3e556f87f1ce03cd28a3837f959",
      "text": "\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=I-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}^{2}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\perp}=-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\,. (B.5)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A4.SS1",
      "start_utf8_byte": 1348892,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 1372363,
      "slice_bytes": 23471,
      "slice_sha256": "22b19e2441e95e4cb6dc49530c7364dc2ff10d0d0b9d8dcfd1347206c98dc8c0",
      "text": "D.1 Hilbert space and Markov operator conventions Definition D.1 (Hilbert space notation) . Let \\zeta be a probability measure. The real Hilbert space L^{2}(\\zeta) consists of equivalence classes of measurable functions f such that \\int f^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta<\\infty . Its inner product and norm are \\langle f,g\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\int fg\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|f\\|_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\langle f,f\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}^{1/2}\\,. (D.1) We write L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta)\\coloneqq\\Bigl\\{f\\in L^{2}(\\zeta):\\int f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta=0\\Bigr\\}\\,. (D.2) When \\zeta=\\pi_{\\eta} , we abbreviate the inner product and norm by \\langle f,g\\rangle and \\|f\\| . Two functions equal \\zeta -almost everywhere represent the same vector. A closed linear subspace \\mathcal{M}\\subseteq L^{2}(\\zeta) has orthogonal complement \\mathcal{M}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq\\{g:\\langle f,g\\rangle=0\\text{ for all }f\\in\\mathcal{M}\\} . Its orthogonal projection is the unique bounded operator \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}} for which \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M} and f-\\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M}^{\\perp} . All operators in the convergence analysis are bounded linear operators on the indicated Hilbert spaces unless explicitly stated otherwise. The adjoint \\mathsf{L}^{*} is characterized by \\langle\\mathsf{L}f,g\\rangle=\\langle f,\\mathsf{L}^{*}g\\rangle . An operator is self-adjoint if \\mathsf{L}=\\mathsf{L}^{*} , a contraction if \\|\\mathsf{L}f\\|\\leq\\|f\\| , and unitary if it is onto and preserves norms. A self-adjoint involution, meaning \\mathsf{L}^{2}=I , is unitary. For self-adjoint operators \\mathsf{L}_{1},\\mathsf{L}_{2} , \\mathsf{L}_{1}\\preceq\\mathsf{L}_{2}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\Longleftrightarrow\\hskip 10.00002pt\\langle f,(\\mathsf{L}_{2}-\\mathsf{L}_{1})f\\rangle\\geq 0\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{for every }f (D.3) is the Loewner order. If \\mathsf{L} is self-adjoint, the spectral theorem defines \\psi(\\mathsf{L}) for bounded Borel functions \\psi ; in particular \\mathbf{1}_{E}(\\mathsf{L}) is the orthogonal projection onto the spectral set E , and a non-negative operator has a unique non-negative square root. Definition D.2 (Markov operators and density evolution) . Let \\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime}) be a Markov kernel preserving \\zeta . Its Markov operator on observables is (\\mathsf{K}f)(z)\\coloneqq\\int f(z^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime})\\,. (D.4) It is a contraction on L^{2}(\\zeta) . Its adjoint \\mathsf{K}^{*} governs density evolution: if a law has density h with respect to \\zeta , then its density after one step is \\mathsf{K}^{*}h . Constants are fixed by both operators. If P\\ll\\zeta has density h , then \\chi^{2}(P\\|\\zeta)=\\|h-1\\|_{\\zeta}^{2}\\,. (D.5) Consequently, an operator norm bound for \\mathsf{K} on L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta) gives the same bound for its adjoint \\mathsf{K}^{*} , and hence gives \\chi^{2} contraction."
    },
    {
      "id": "A3.E4",
      "start_utf8_byte": 1029755,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 1031371,
      "slice_bytes": 1616,
      "slice_sha256": "832e5eba8dbe8fd2b0361f4514814d7e6462b20418b04693badb9aa6122ce341",
      "text": "\\|\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}f\\|\\leq\\frac{1-\\alpha\\eta}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,\\|f\\|=\\rho_{\\rm mac}\\,\\|f\\|\\,. (C.4)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E10",
      "start_utf8_byte": 768975,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 770419,
      "slice_bytes": 1444,
      "slice_sha256": "7b09ee8258f3e6c4ecc506e9513ec8256e0d1f4083f38b545d6c1dd03486c0fb",
      "text": "\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\coloneqq(I-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{on }\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\,. (B.10)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E11",
      "start_utf8_byte": 771531,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 773654,
      "slice_bytes": 2123,
      "slice_sha256": "6d19997456600e603e6f081f055bf929069a5e636c8c8fa7d2cb37a8615428f7",
      "text": "\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}^{2}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}f\\|=\\|\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}f\\|\\,,\\hskip 10.00002ptf\\in\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\,. (B.11)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E15",
      "start_utf8_byte": 783959,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 785610,
      "slice_bytes": 1651,
      "slice_sha256": "0a85d657415c5c8dbb19e6014e528156f3ce5badc750e5849423f1f042da3ebf",
      "text": "\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}=(I-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\\succeq\\gamma_{\\rm gap}I\\hskip 20.00003pt\\text{on }\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P},0}\\,. (B.15)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E16",
      "start_utf8_byte": 790549,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 793315,
      "slice_bytes": 2766,
      "slice_sha256": "a6725987d1979361ae4770ec893b554213101161e297c456081d28b8ef5b9042",
      "text": "\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\coloneqq\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\\,:\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P},0}\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=I_{\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P},0}}\\,. (B.16)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.SS2.p5",
      "start_utf8_byte": 789452,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 795301,
      "slice_bytes": 5849,
      "slice_sha256": "2806c44543820aa78989755ce7b284dd2da96c38c5013d193b1d323d64ca864e",
      "text": "Since \\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}} is boundedly invertible on \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P},0} , ( B.11 ) gives the polar decomposition \\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\coloneqq\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\\,:\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P},0}\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=I_{\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P},0}}\\,. (B.16) Here \\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}} measures the size of the microscopic component created by reflection, while the isometry \\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}} records its direction in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} . The next proposition says that the half-turn acts nearly as -I on the corresponding microscopic directions \\mathsf{V}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{\\Gamma}_{\\mathsf{P}}^{-1}f ."
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E9",
      "start_utf8_byte": 765724,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 768134,
      "slice_bytes": 2410,
      "slice_sha256": "d24fec695423a3d6af884a8a3fe3c46804b6e817f935fb89b1853a6ab5f65241",
      "text": "(\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}f)(y)=\\mathbb{E}[f(Y_{-})\\mid Y_{+}=y]\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}f\\|^{2}=\\mathbb{E}\\Var(f(Y_{-})\\mid Y_{+})\\,. (B.9)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E12",
      "start_utf8_byte": 775053,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 776847,
      "slice_bytes": 1794,
      "slice_sha256": "7925618a142c1514a962d18e4c580f39fa4bc85426ba98de4c84a8b90e6bdc42",
      "text": "\\rho_{\\rm mac}\\coloneqq\\frac{1-\\alpha\\eta}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\gamma_{\\rm gap}\\coloneqq\\sqrt{1-\\rho_{\\rm mac}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\alpha\\eta}}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,. (B.12)"
    },
    {
      "id": "S1.p1",
      "start_utf8_byte": 47126,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 50729,
      "slice_bytes": 3603,
      "slice_sha256": "a9bc657fb926368561ab2657dfbfbf6a7a0ba5b8c2122f8d1fb8510a0a975262",
      "text": "We study the problem of sampling from the probability measure \\mu(\\mathrm{d}x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x on \\mathbb{R}^{d} , where V\\in C^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{d}) satisfies, for 0<\\alpha\\leq\\beta , \\alpha I\\preceq\\nabla^{2}V(x)\\preceq\\beta I\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\kappa\\coloneqq\\tfrac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\,. (1.1) The main result of this paper is the following."
    },
    {
      "id": "S2.E6",
      "start_utf8_byte": 96222,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 98215,
      "slice_bytes": 1993,
      "slice_sha256": "1f01e27c8eb38fd05a57d820c65ee6b36832171cce845e8cd1b52f4588526375",
      "text": "\\pi_{\\eta}(\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V(x)-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^{2}}{2\\eta}\\Bigr\\}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,. (2.6)"
    },
    {
      "id": "S2.E7",
      "start_utf8_byte": 98826,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 100299,
      "slice_bytes": 1473,
      "slice_sha256": "2be554d84baf1f2b796ade05fba49271b5d05e4e8d2d46427d47a6756f85c8f9",
      "text": "X\\sim\\mu\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptZ\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)\\ \\text{independently}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptY=X+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,Z\\,. (2.7)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.SS1.p1",
      "start_utf8_byte": 708364,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 727705,
      "slice_bytes": 19341,
      "slice_sha256": "395059e86bc44a9581440d09e7095e1979f6579e14a55979f5bd2fa382b6fb24",
      "text": "Our target distribution is the joint law \\pi_{\\eta} defined in ( 2.6 ), and we set \\mathfrak{H}\\coloneqq L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}) . Recall from ( 3.11 ) that the ideal Proximal BPS has transition kernel \\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)=\\delta_{2x-y}(\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime})\\,\\bigl[\\rho\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})+(1-\\rho)\\,\\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}}(x,\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})\\bigr]\\,, where \\mathcal{H}_{y^{\\prime}} is the conditional half-turn kernel from Proposition 3.2 . Since the algorithm is a discrete-time chain, its evolution is described by the induced one-step Markov operator on observables, (\\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\\prime},y^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}\\bigl((x,y),\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}y^{\\prime}\\bigr)\\,. Thus \\mathcal{K} acts on probability laws, whereas \\mathsf{K} is its induced action on observables. The first term in brackets in \\mathcal{K} is the conditional resampling branch. After the auxiliary coordinate is updated to y^{\\prime} , this branch replaces the x -coordinate by an independent draw from \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y^{\\prime}} . The associated conditional expectation operator is (\\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq\\int f(x^{\\prime},y)\\,\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}(\\mathrm{d}x^{\\prime})=\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y=y]\\,. (B.1) In particular, \\mathsf{P}f depends only on the coordinate y . The range and kernel of \\mathsf{P} are \\begin{split}\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\\text{ for some }g\\in L^{2}(\\pi_{\\eta}^{Y})\\}\\,,\\\\ \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\ker(\\mathsf{P})=\\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp})=\\{f\\in\\mathfrak{H}:\\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\\mid Y]=0\\}\\,.\\end{split} (B.2) Here \\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq I-\\mathsf{P} , so \\mathfrak{H}=\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\oplus\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} and f=\\mathsf{P}f+\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}f . In the common terminology of hypocoercivity [ 14 , 21 ] , \\operatorname{ran}(\\mathsf{P}) is the macroscopic subspace and its orthogonal complement is the microscopic subspace. Thus functions in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} depend only on the coordinate y , whereas functions in \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} have zero mean under \\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y} for \\pi_{\\eta}^{Y} -almost every y ."
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.SS1.p2",
      "start_utf8_byte": 727706,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 733595,
      "slice_bytes": 5889,
      "slice_sha256": "867f37d31c89be99b7218d384e7e1eafec857b4f0da5f51191f6f314828f91c0",
      "text": "Relative to the decomposition \\mathfrak{H}=\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\\oplus\\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} , we write an operator \\mathsf{T} on \\mathfrak{H} in block form as \\mathsf{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}&\\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\perp}\\\\ \\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}&\\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\perp}\\end{pmatrix}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\begin{aligned} \\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}\\,,&\\mathsf{T}_{\\mathsf{P}\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\,,\\\\ \\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}\\,,&\\mathsf{T}_{\\perp\\perp}&\\coloneqq\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\mathsf{T}\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\,.\\end{aligned} (B.3) The first subscript specifies the target subspace and the second the source subspace; thus \\mathsf{T}_{ab}:\\mathfrak{H}_{b}\\to\\mathfrak{H}_{a} for a,b\\in\\{\\mathsf{P},\\perp\\} ."
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.SS1.p3",
      "start_utf8_byte": 733596,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 742298,
      "slice_bytes": 8702,
      "slice_sha256": "654bd2e3d43cfbb9332da21b4b76c2066f0c7aa4275c5a736d6bb2a6f1552873",
      "text": "We first apply this block notation to the reflection map \\mathsf{U} from ( 2.14 ). We use the same symbol for its pullback on observables: (\\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)\\coloneqq f(\\mathsf{U}(x,y))=f(x,2x-y)\\,. (B.4) This pullback is self-adjoint and unitary, and hence has block matrix \\mathsf{U}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}&\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\\\ \\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}&\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\perp}\\end{pmatrix}\\,. Here \\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}=\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{U}\\mathsf{P} maps \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} to itself, whereas \\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=\\mathsf{P}^{\\perp}\\mathsf{U}\\mathsf{P} maps \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} to \\mathfrak{H}_{\\perp} . Since \\mathsf{U} is self-adjoint and unitary, \\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}} is a self-adjoint contraction on \\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}} . Writing \\mathsf{U}^{2}=I in blocks gives \\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=I-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}^{2}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\perp}=-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\,. (B.5)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.E8",
      "start_utf8_byte": 762524,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 764253,
      "slice_bytes": 1729,
      "slice_sha256": "5a60b7a7667ec1a06d63f370f1711890038e27cb223b63d17031494e7ee0928a",
      "text": "Y_{+}\\coloneqq X+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,Z\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptY_{-}\\coloneqq X-\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,Z\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptZ\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptX\\sim\\mu\\,. (B.8)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A3.SS1.p2",
      "start_utf8_byte": 1004189,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 1009595,
      "slice_bytes": 5406,
      "slice_sha256": "96d3f106bcd0b32f60a5c97b7df3d8f231c571d286884e8ca4aa30cf3b64749b",
      "text": "Recall that the conditional density p_{y} of Y_{-} given Y_{+}=y is p_{y}(u)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V((y+u)/2)-\\frac{\\|y-u\\|^{2}}{8\\eta}\\Bigr\\}\\,, and define s_{y}(u)\\coloneqq-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\nabla V((y+u)/2)-\\frac{y-u}{4\\eta}\\,. Differentiating the normalized conditional density gives \\nabla\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}f(y)=\\mathrm{Cov}\\bigl(f(Y_{-}),s_{y}(Y_{-})\\bigm|Y_{+}=y\\bigr)\\,. (C.1)"
    },
    {
      "id": "A2.Thmtheorem1.p1",
      "start_utf8_byte": 774554,
      "end_utf8_byte_exclusive": 777520,
      "slice_bytes": 2966,
      "slice_sha256": "3ecbbc427d67888cc1fc83578053d55b218d901d0a231cd9aa29b037f61288ec",
      "text": "Assume \\beta\\eta\\leq 1 , and set \\rho_{\\rm mac}\\coloneqq\\frac{1-\\alpha\\eta}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\gamma_{\\rm gap}\\coloneqq\\sqrt{1-\\rho_{\\rm mac}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\alpha\\eta}}{1+\\alpha\\eta}\\,. (B.12) Note that \\gamma_{\\rm gap}\\geq\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\alpha\\eta} . Then the following hold."
    }
  ],
  "all23_primary_raw_slices_checked": true,
  "primary_not_copied": true,
  "source_only": true
}
